WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.900
Film Courage: Can you think of an example
where you have written yourself into a corner?

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.560
And how did you write yourself out?

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.470
Jeffrey Reddick, horror screenwriter: Yeah…pretty
easy.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:14.349
TAMARA was a movie I’d written myself into
a corner because the though thing in horror

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:20.490
films you make your villain strong and it’s
like you have to come up with a way to stop

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.930
them and you don’t want to do something
super generic but you know TAMARA was sort

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:30.170
of my homage to CARRIE about a girl who is
picked on and bullied and these kids pull

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:35.160
a prank on her and accident kill her and in
the script you know you is supposed to be

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.140
really frumpy and unattractive and then they
bury her in the woods and the next Monday

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.480
she comes back really smokin’ hot and she
knows all their secrets and she uses that

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.059
to turn against them.

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:51.720
But I made her, she was really powerful so
I’m like if they stop her they’re either

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.800
going to have to find a spell (since she’s
into witchcraft) they are going to have to

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.900
find a spell to stop her which is kind of
(we’ve seen that a hundred times).

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.160
And I made her so strong that you can’t
kill her because she came back from the dead,

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.489
so what do I do?

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:10.040
And if you haven’t seen TAMARA (a little
spoiler).

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.310
So basically I tied her fate to the teacher
that she was in love with, so he ended up

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:22.539
doing a heroic sacrifice, grabs Tamara and
jumps off the building with her, sacrifices

00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:23.600
himself to stop her.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.259
So heroic sacrifice is always a way to end
a good horror film, to get yourself out of

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:30.259
a corner.

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:33.290
But when you create a monster, you have to
find a way to kill it and then it’s like

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.759
you don’t want it to be something overly-complicated.

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:40.560
You don’t want it to be as simple as “Oh,
we’ll just get a spell and kill her.”

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.359
So yeah, I definitely wrote myself into a
corner with that movie and I was just like

00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:50.950
“Well, I’ll just have the teacher sacrifice
his life to stop her.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:56.349
Film Courage: And then when you got notes
(let’s say) the first few times around showing

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.639
the [the script], what were the notes?

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:01.239
What was the feedback about Tamara?

00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:03.079
Was she too much this?

00:02:03.079 --> 00:02:04.279
Not enough this?

00:02:04.279 --> 00:02:12.879
Jeffrey: Tamara was probably the easiest,
that was probably the easiest project to as

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:18.540
far as we found a studio that wanted to do
it and they did it, it was a pretty easy sell.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.329
Because I wrote that one because I wanted
to have fun with a movie, because everyone

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:24.790
had been like “Bring us something like FINAL
DESTINATION, bring us something…”

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.790
And then you bring something and it’s like
“Oh, that’s too much like FINAL DESTINATION.”

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:29.790
And it’s like “Ugh.”

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.769
So finally I just wanted to write something
fun.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:43.430
And in my head I wanted to write like a fun,
quotable, campy, sexy, horror movie.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:49.060
I mean it turned out really well, I did joke
that because of the budget we had a certain

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:56.129
budget and then magically half that budget
disappeared before the movie started shooting.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:03:00.659
So we didn’t have the money that we went
into the movie having.

00:03:00.659 --> 00:03:06.460
So I always kind of joke it’s like the PG-13,
ABC family version of an R-rated horror film

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.460
that I wrote.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.140
So when you watch it, you can definitely see
like a lot of places where the story got kind

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.950
of chopped out a little bit and stuff didn’t
go like “Oh, they could have really pushed

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.150
the envelope here and they played it really
safe.”

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:22.360
And that was all because of budget stuff.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.810
So that was another one where I actually wrote
a book with a friend of mine (J.D.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:32.200
Matthews) we put it out based on the screenplay
just so people could see what the original

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.859
story was like because the only thing we had
to change on that because they was some really

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:40.200
intense stuff in there but nobody really said
to take it out and that’s what is always

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.079
funny because often times it’s the studio
that wants you to take out stuff and play

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:47.950
it safe and a couple of movies where that
has happened to me and it hasn’t been the

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:57.969
studio that has done it, either a producer
kind of running off with some money or someone

00:03:57.969 --> 00:04:02.189
on the movie set going “Oh, let’s tone
this down.”

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:05.640
So I’ve had the opposite of what a lot of
people have which is where the studio wants

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.640
to play it safe.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.719
Like I’ve had a couple movies where I’m
like I can’t believe they are letting me

00:04:08.719 --> 00:04:09.719
get away with this.

00:04:09.719 --> 00:04:13.609
And then you get on set and one of the actors
is like “Oh, I don’t want to do this.”

00:04:13.609 --> 00:04:15.610
And I’m like “Dammit!

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.470
Usually the studio stops me not some actor.”

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:23.810
Again, it’s a very interesting business
that we’re in.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.419
Film Courage: So having FINAL DESTINATION
and the various ones that you were a part

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:34.229
of under your belt, do you think that sometimes
it’s difficult because they pigeonhole you

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:38.740
with that and you want to do something in
a little bit of a different realm?

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.090
Or is it nice because you’re known for that
so then they have confidence [in your ability]?

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:49.470
Jeffrey: I mean for me it’s nice because
I’m a horror fan (I have science fiction

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.010
kind of, horror overtones to it, but a big
kind of science fiction project we are doing).

00:04:54.010 --> 00:05:00.849
But I might branch out a little bit but I’m
very happy writing horror (I love the genre

00:05:00.849 --> 00:05:06.629
so) and so part of it growing up was like
you would read in Fangoria Magazine which

00:05:06.629 --> 00:05:09.819
is like which is like The Bible for horror
people, you would read directors and they’d

00:05:09.819 --> 00:05:13.710
be like “Well, it’s not really a horror
film, it’s more of a supernatural thriller.”

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.910
And they would try to say everything but it’s
a horror film and then they would make a horror

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.069
film because they would probably make a profit
and then they would quit making horror and

00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:22.350
go off and make other stuff.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.935
So a lot of directors are like “Well, this
is a stepping-stone to go off and do what

00:05:24.935 --> 00:05:25.935
I want to do.”

00:05:25.935 --> 00:05:29.580
And so growing up you’d get like “Arrgghh,
screw you.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.639
This is a great genre.”

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:36.530
And so this is something I embrace wholly.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:40.210
I love going to horror conventions and it’s
a lot of fun.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.870
There are a lot of fans, it’s a great fanbase.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:49.210
And a lot of the people I work with (my peers)
were all horror fans like me and we grew up

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:53.841
loving the same stuff and watching the same
stuff so it’s a really fun community to

00:05:53.841 --> 00:05:53.860
be a part of.

